1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,504
<font color="#000000">From the University of Tokyo, NeurIPS 2022: the
Langevin autoencoder.</font>

2
00:00:04,584 --> 00:00:08,588
<font color="#000000">Langevin dynamics samples the intractable
posteriors of deep latent models,</font>

3
00:00:08,668 --> 00:00:12,672
<font color="#000000">but slowly.</font>

4
00:00:12,752 --> 00:00:16,756
<font color="#000000">The authors amortize it into a shared encoder,
prove it valid MCMC,</font>

5
00:00:16,836 --> 00:00:20,840
<font color="#000000">and beat variational autoencoders.</font>

6
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:28,248
<font color="#000000">Training deep latent variable models by maximum
likelihood needs an expectation over the latent posterior,</font>

7
00:00:28,328 --> 00:00:29,656
<font color="#000000">which is intractable.</font>

8
00:00:29,736 --> 00:00:33,144
<font color="#000000">Langevin dynamics samples that posterior accurately,</font>

9
00:00:33,224 --> 00:00:37,100
<font color="#000000">but runs a fresh chain per data point and converges
slowly.</font>

10
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:46,018
<font color="#000000">Variational inference dominates thanks to
amortization: one shared encoder predicts the latents for all data.</font>

11
00:00:46,098 --> 00:00:50,446
<font color="#000000">But it relies on tractable Gaussians, limiting accuracy.</font>

12
00:00:50,526 --> 00:00:55,269
<font color="#000000">MCMC has no such limit, yet nobody truly amortized
it before.</font>

13
00:00:55,349 --> 00:00:59,460
<font color="#000000">The goal: amortized efficiency with MCMC flexibility.</font>

14
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:02,711
<font color="#000000">Two contributions.</font>

15
00:01:02,791 --> 00:01:05,663
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

16
00:01:05,743 --> 00:01:08,614
<font color="#000000">amortized Langevin dynamics: an MCMC algorithm that
replaces per-datapoint iterations</font>

17
00:01:08,694 --> 00:01:11,566
<font color="#000000">with Langevin updates on a shared encoder's parameters,</font>

18
00:01:11,646 --> 00:01:14,517
<font color="#000000">proven valid.</font>

19
00:01:14,597 --> 00:01:17,469
<font color="#000000">Second, the Langevin autoencoder,</font>

20
00:01:17,549 --> 00:01:20,420
<font color="#000000">a generative model that is a small tweak of a
standard autoencoder.</font>

21
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:25,597
<font color="#000000">The key idea: move randomness from latent space to
encoder parameters.</font>

22
00:01:25,677 --> 00:01:29,289
<font color="#000000">A deterministic encoder maps observations to latents;</font>

23
00:01:29,369 --> 00:01:32,215
<font color="#000000">updating its parameters moves the samples.</font>

24
00:01:32,295 --> 00:01:39,112
<font color="#000000">Convergence is guaranteed when a fixed feature
extractor feeds a linear layer wider than the batch.</font>

25
00:01:39,192 --> 00:01:43,292
<font color="#000000">The autoencoder runs a few steps before each
decoder update,</font>

26
00:01:43,372 --> 00:01:45,800
<font color="#000000">optionally with Metropolis-Hastings.</font>

27
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:50,064
<font color="#000000">Validation comes in two stages.</font>

28
00:01:50,144 --> 00:01:54,028
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

29
00:01:54,108 --> 00:01:57,992
<font color="#000000">toy problems with known answers: a
bivariate-Gaussian posterior and a random-network posterior.</font>

30
00:01:58,072 --> 00:02:01,956
<font color="#000000">Then image generation on MNIST, S-V-H-N, CIFAR-10,
and CelebA,</font>

31
00:02:02,036 --> 00:02:05,920
<font color="#000000">scored by negative ELBO per dimension over three seeds.</font>

32
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:10,362
<font color="#000000">On toy problems, amortized Langevin dynamics
reproduces multimodal,</font>

33
00:02:10,442 --> 00:02:14,504
<font color="#000000">correlated posteriors that variational inference misses.</font>

34
00:02:14,584 --> 00:02:18,646
<font color="#000000">On images,</font>

35
00:02:18,726 --> 00:02:22,788
<font color="#000000">the Langevin autoencoder achieves the lowest
negative ELBO on all four datasets.</font>

36
00:02:22,868 --> 00:02:26,930
<font color="#000000">The lesson: more accurate posterior sampling yields
better models.</font>

37
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:30,906
<font color="#000000">Two ablations.</font>

38
00:02:30,986 --> 00:02:34,582
<font color="#000000">Encoder capacity confirms the theory: when the
final linear layer is at least the batch size,</font>

39
00:02:34,662 --> 00:02:38,258
<font color="#000000">samples match; smaller, some collapse.</font>

40
00:02:38,338 --> 00:02:41,934
<font color="#000000">On images, the Metropolis-Hastings step stabilizes
training,</font>

41
00:02:42,014 --> 00:02:45,610
<font color="#000000">while iteration count barely matters beyond two.</font>

42
00:02:45,990 --> 00:02:48,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">The gains are consistent but modest.</font>

43
00:02:48,889 --> 00:02:53,561
<font color="#FFFFFF">On MNIST, negative ELBO drops to one point one
seven seven,</font>

44
00:02:53,641 --> 00:02:57,105
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus one point one eight nine for the VAE.</font>

45
00:02:57,185 --> 00:03:02,099
<font color="#FFFFFF">CIFAR-10, CelebA, and S-V-H-N show similar small
improvements.</font>

46
00:03:02,179 --> 00:03:06,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">The cost: about two-and-a-quarter times slower than a VAE.</font>

47
00:03:07,150 --> 00:03:11,407
<font color="#000000">The takeaway is elegant: move Langevin noise from
the latents to the encoder's parameters,</font>

48
00:03:11,488 --> 00:03:15,745
<font color="#000000">so sampling becomes both efficient and flexible.</font>

49
00:03:15,825 --> 00:03:20,083
<font color="#000000">Provably valid,</font>

50
00:03:20,163 --> 00:03:24,420
<font color="#000000">it looks almost like a standard autoencoder yet
consistently beats variational autoencoders on test likelihood.</font>
